1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,780
From the mammoth to the mini and by the billions, they're the driving force behind much of our modern existence.

2
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:18,680
Just bundle the right metals and caustic chemicals and you've harnessed the power to go zero to 60 in four seconds.

3
00:00:18,681 --> 00:00:23,520
This car is powered by 6,831 lithium-ion batteries.

4
00:00:23,521 --> 00:00:30,380
Or explore distant worlds and maybe someday send a probe on a fantastic voyage into your bloodstream.

5
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:34,780
It's time to get a charge out of batteries on Modern Marbles.

6
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:56,620
When you need a portable, convenient power source, count on this unassuming yet indispensable

7
00:00:56,621 --> 00:01:03,620
wonder to make your tech, tools, and toys surge with energy.

8
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:10,120
Americans alone purchase nearly three billion dry cell batteries every year.

9
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:13,500
And that doesn't take into account all the special batteries built into devices

10
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:17,420
including computers and cell phones.

11
00:01:17,421 --> 00:01:20,260
Sure, some batteries lead a rather quiet existence,

12
00:01:20,261 --> 00:01:22,940
like those inside watches and clocks,

13
00:01:22,941 --> 00:01:24,980
providing tick after tick year after year.

14
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,540
But others live fast and die young,

15
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:32,440
like those powering smart bomb guidance systems.

16
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:37,180
We all depend on batteries.

17
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,880
But in some cases, people's lives depend on them.

18
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,680
Dick Cheney's heart keeps on pumping,

19
00:01:44,681 --> 00:01:46,840
thanks to the battery inside his pacemaker.

20
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,360
When you come down to it,

21
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:51,880
just about everything requiring electrical power

22
00:01:51,881 --> 00:01:55,820
that isn't plugged in or attached to a power source contains a battery.

23
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:00,800
From the remote control you've no doubt got clutched in your hand

24
00:02:00,801 --> 00:02:05,540
to the high-definition cameras recording the action for this episode of Modern Marvels.

25
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,500
Batteries don't contain electricity,

26
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:12,060
but rather the potential for electricity in the form of chemicals.

27
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:15,800
A textbook definition is a battery is a device

28
00:02:15,801 --> 00:02:19,140
that converts chemical energy directly into electrical energy

29
00:02:19,141 --> 00:02:21,540
by means of an oxidation-reduction reaction.

30
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:26,880
Batteries now come in a vast array of sizes and chemical compositions,

31
00:02:26,881 --> 00:02:31,880
but all have some configuration of these basic parts.

32
00:02:31,881 --> 00:02:35,880
They have two electrodes, each made with a different metal.

33
00:02:35,881 --> 00:02:38,880
The positively charged electrode is called the cathode,

34
00:02:38,881 --> 00:02:42,880
and the negatively charged electrode is the anode.

35
00:02:42,881 --> 00:02:46,880
A liquid paste or gel electrolyte must contain an acid or alkaline

36
00:02:46,881 --> 00:02:50,880
that's caustic enough to cause a chemical reaction in the metals.

37
00:02:50,881 --> 00:02:55,880
This reaction creates an exchange of electrons between the anode and the cathode.

38
00:02:55,881 --> 00:02:59,880
But only when one or more batteries are placed in a device like a flashlight,

39
00:02:59,881 --> 00:03:04,880
and the circuit is closed by turning it on, does the electricity begin to flow.

40
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:12,880
The anode that's in the middle of cylindrical batteries is oxidized, causing a release of electrons.

41
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:17,880
They're drawn through the circuit by the cathode that's undergoing a reduction reaction.

42
00:03:17,881 --> 00:03:24,260
reaction. As the electrons travel through the circuit, they provide the electricity

43
00:03:24,261 --> 00:03:25,833
to power the device.

44
00:03:25,834 --> 00:03:31,180
Scientifically speaking, a single unit capable of supplying power is

45
00:03:31,181 --> 00:03:33,060
referred to as a cell.

46
00:03:33,061 --> 00:03:36,168
A standard AA is actually a single cell.

47
00:03:36,169 --> 00:03:39,820
It's only when you put cells together that they

48
00:03:39,821 --> 00:03:42,120
form what is called a battery.

49
00:03:42,121 --> 00:03:43,208
So AA's are cells.

50
00:03:43,209 --> 00:03:47,560
But here's why it's correct to call one of those little rectangular ones

51
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:48,560
a 9-volt battery.

52
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:50,940
A 9-volt battery is rectangular in shape

53
00:03:50,941 --> 00:03:54,040
because it actually contains six individual unit cells.

54
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:57,300
Each of these unit cells is 1.5 volts,

55
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,600
thus providing 9 volts to the battery.

56
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:05,160
The more cells we connect, the greater the voltage.

57
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:08,420
And there's no limit to how many cells can be linked together.

58
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:11,760
In Fairbanks, Alaska,

59
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:14,740
engineers have pushed this principle to the extreme,

60
00:04:14,741 --> 00:04:18,980
creating the world's most powerful battery.

61
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,300
The signs outside give you fair warning.

62
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:27,100
Inside, if you touch this brawny battery system in the wrong place,

63
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:30,140
it could mean a 5,000-volt jolt.

64
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:35,000
That's two and a half times greater than the average electric chair.

65
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:38,180
The battery energy storage system, or BEZ as we call it,

66
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:42,000
is housed in a building 500 feet by 120 feet wide.

67
00:04:42,001 --> 00:04:45,586
We have four strings of batteries.

68
00:04:45,587 --> 00:04:53,180
Each string contains 3,440 cells, so a total of 13,760 individual cells.

69
00:04:54,760 --> 00:05:01,780
Fairbanks and its surroundings need this monstrous battery to let loose with its stored energy during potentially crippling power outages.

70
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:08,620
No power lines link Alaska and the lower 48 states.

71
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:14,160
And only one line extends from Anchorage, the nearest large city, to Fairbanks.

72
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:20,440
During the long, dark, and bitterly cold winters, Fairbanks is especially vulnerable.

73
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:24,740
During the winter, temperature extremes, say it's 40 below, say it's 50 below.

74
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:27,660
At those temperatures, any kind of an outage becomes very critical,

75
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:31,780
because a house at those temperatures can freeze within four to five hours.

76
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:35,920
The water pipe's inside, the heating system inside, if there's no electricity to it.

77
00:05:35,921 --> 00:05:42,920
Bez is designed to discharge a whopping 27 megawatts of power for only 15 minutes.

78
00:05:42,921 --> 00:05:46,920
Enough time for the electric company's dispatchers to remedy the problem,

79
00:05:46,921 --> 00:05:50,920
or for customers to start their own generators.

80
00:05:50,921 --> 00:05:57,920
Each of the 1,376 black boxes on the shelves contain 10 cells apiece.

81
00:05:57,921 --> 00:06:04,920
The electrodes in Bez's 13,760 cells are made from nickel and carbon.

82
00:06:04,921 --> 00:06:06,400
made from nickel and cadmium.

83
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:12,380
NICAD's cells are both cost-effective and long-lasting.

84
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:16,020
We expect a shelf or actually operating life of these batteries

85
00:06:16,021 --> 00:06:17,380
to be in excess of 20 years.

86
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:21,700
Nickel-cadmium cells are rechargeable,

87
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:25,740
and that means that we can charge and discharge them

88
00:06:25,741 --> 00:06:28,820
hundreds of times, even thousands of times,

89
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:30,700
depending on how we use them.

90
00:06:30,701 --> 00:06:34,580
And with this battery, it's actually sitting on

91
00:06:34,581 --> 00:06:36,980
what we call standby.

92
00:06:36,981 --> 00:06:39,380
That means most of the time it's just

93
00:06:39,381 --> 00:06:42,380
drawing enough energy from the grid

94
00:06:42,381 --> 00:06:44,760
to keep it in a fully charged state.

95
00:06:44,761 --> 00:06:47,620
And it's just waiting for that one moment

96
00:06:47,621 --> 00:06:49,860
when something happens on the grid

97
00:06:49,861 --> 00:06:54,500
and the battery needs to discharge in a hurry.

98
00:06:54,501 --> 00:06:56,940
Like all batteries, our batteries are DC powered.

99
00:06:56,941 --> 00:07:00,440
And we gather all that power and focus it on our DC bus,

100
00:07:00,441 --> 00:07:03,440
which comes into this room here in the background.

101
00:07:03,441 --> 00:07:07,000
We have a positive bus and a negative bus,

102
00:07:07,001 --> 00:07:09,280
5,000 volts sitting here between these two pieces

103
00:07:09,281 --> 00:07:10,660
of aluminum bus.

104
00:07:10,661 --> 00:07:13,340
It is then fed into this device called a converter.

105
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:15,880
The converter takes the DC power and converts it

106
00:07:15,881 --> 00:07:21,140
into AC power, which is then fed out on these pipes above here.

107
00:07:21,141 --> 00:07:23,320
The pipes carry the power out of the building

108
00:07:23,321 --> 00:07:26,840
and into a transformer that steps up the 5,000 volts

109
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:31,280
to 138,000 volts.

110
00:07:31,281 --> 00:07:36,960
The power enters an adjacent substation and flows out to the grid, serving Fairbanks and the surrounding area.

111
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:40,500
We've been extremely happy with how the BEZ has performed.

112
00:07:40,501 --> 00:07:48,280
Over the last four years, we've seen 217 events, and we have eliminated or prevented over a million customer outages.

113
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:52,480
That's very interesting because we only have 42,000 customers.

114
00:07:53,800 --> 00:08:00,280
While Big BEZ is an elaborate and complicated system, the most basic of batteries can be surprisingly low-tech.

115
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:04,400
Batteries are actually very simple to demonstrate.

116
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:08,320
And we can do it with something as simple as a lemon and a few household items.

117
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:12,460
So, for instance, we can take a galvanized nail, that's a nail coated with zinc,

118
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:16,640
and insert it in the lemon, which will serve as our electrochemical cell.

119
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:20,480
And in a small slit here next to the nail, we'll insert a penny.

120
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:24,960
And the electrons tend to flow from the zinc to the copper,

121
00:08:24,961 --> 00:08:27,360
which really has a desire to pick up the electrons.

122
00:08:27,361 --> 00:08:36,440
And in order to make the battery work, all we have to do is connect the two leads to something that's a load and serves as an outlet for useful work.

123
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:39,260
And we can demonstrate that with a clock.

124
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:44,180
Here we have a clock composed of four different lemons, so we have a four-lemon clock.

125
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:53,567
If you're looking for a power source that's a little less sour, you can always opt for the smaller, uniformly sized, and durable varieties made by battery

126
00:08:53,568 --> 00:08:54,340
manufacturers.

127
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:04,740
Energizer, one of the industry's leaders, is a direct descendant of the very first consumer battery, called the Columbia.

128
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:10,680
The Columbia was introduced in 1896 by the National Carbon Company.

129
00:09:11,380 --> 00:09:16,560
In 1898, an inventor, Conrad Huber, came up with a flashlight.

130
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:23,080
National Carbon Company, the manufacturer of batteries, and Conrad Huber's company, the manufacturer of flashlights,

131
00:09:23,081 --> 00:09:26,220
decided to merge, forming the Ever Ready Battery Company.

132
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:34,480
In the 1970s, Everettie created the Energizer Company to market its higher-end alkaline batteries.

133
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:40,640
And with the help of an iconic pink percussionist, its business kept growing and growing and growing.

134
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:50,640
Energizer's sprawling North Carolina facility is the largest alkaline battery manufacturing and packaging complex in the world.

135
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:54,000
The manufacturing facility here in Ashborough, North Carolina,

136
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:57,960
is more than a million square feet, employing more than a thousand people.

137
00:09:57,961 --> 00:10:03,320
We make AA's, AAA's, 9 volts, but today we're going to focus on D batteries

138
00:10:03,321 --> 00:10:05,780
because we make more than anyone else.

139
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:11,120
The North Carolina plant produces about a million D cells every day.

140
00:10:14,180 --> 00:10:18,500
Energizer's first step is to send the steel cans it buys from outside vendors

141
00:10:18,501 --> 00:10:22,080
through a washer and dryer to remove any residues or contaminants.

142
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:31,800
Elsewhere in the facility, a forklift dumps huge bags of manganese dioxide into a hopper

143
00:10:31,801 --> 00:10:34,440
that feeds the material into blenders,

144
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:38,720
where it's mixed with graphite and other ingredients that make up the cathode material.

145
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:45,720
The cathode mix is now fed downstairs to the can-filling machine.

146
00:10:45,721 --> 00:10:53,160
The cathode mix fills the steel can and it makes its way on to the next step of the process, which is cathode molding.

147
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:58,320
Where the cathode mix is molded to the inner diameter of the steel can.

148
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:04,680
This is a 100-ton press that does four cans at a time.

149
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:08,200
Next comes the separator material.

150
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:15,560
In these D-cells and in all batteries, the cathode and the anode must be kept from touching each other to prevent short circuits.

151
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:22,720
These machines insert two layers of a special paper that form a protective lining, keeping the two electrodes separate.

152
00:11:22,721 --> 00:11:28,720
Now with the separator in place, it's time to add the anode and the electrolyte.

153
00:11:28,721 --> 00:11:35,720
The anode is basically a zinc slurry, and the electrolyte's alkaline ingredient is potassium hydroxide.

154
00:11:35,721 --> 00:11:40,720
Even though this type of battery is called a dry cell, that's somewhat of a misnomer.

155
00:11:40,721 --> 00:11:41,421
misnomer.

156
00:11:41,422 --> 00:11:46,720
A wet cell contains free-flowing electrolyte, whereas a dry cell contains a

157
00:11:46,721 --> 00:11:49,085
gel or paste-like electrolyte.

158
00:11:49,086 --> 00:11:53,420
Once the anode is in place, in the machinery behind me,

159
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:57,953
the collector assembly is inserted into the can.

160
00:11:57,954 --> 00:12:01,780
The nail or current collector is inserted into

161
00:12:01,781 --> 00:12:04,959
the anode, thus completing the circuit.

162
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:09,280
The next set of machines places and crimps the bottom

163
00:12:09,281 --> 00:12:15,560
covers onto the cans, creating the negative end of the cells.

164
00:12:15,561 --> 00:12:21,040
Then the cans are inverted and fed into a machine that welds the top covers into place.

165
00:12:21,041 --> 00:12:24,680
These are the positive ends of the cells.

166
00:12:24,681 --> 00:12:29,200
And if you were wondering, there are reasons why D-cells and other standard consumer batteries

167
00:12:29,201 --> 00:12:30,200
are cylindrical.

168
00:12:30,201 --> 00:12:34,600
The key reason why batteries are made in a cylindrical shape is due to the strength

169
00:12:34,601 --> 00:12:37,100
and durability of the container.

170
00:12:37,101 --> 00:12:41,560
The cylinder allows for an even distribution of internal pressures, which allows for a

171
00:12:41,561 --> 00:12:44,940
more effective sealing system.

172
00:12:44,941 --> 00:12:48,280
Now we're at the final assembly step.

173
00:12:48,281 --> 00:12:52,560
This is where we label the product.

174
00:12:52,561 --> 00:12:58,700
Once it's been labeled, the label is heat shrunk onto the cell.

175
00:12:58,701 --> 00:13:04,540
Every battery is visually inspected to ensure the label is in the proper place.

176
00:13:04,541 --> 00:13:12,580
It is voltage tested and current tested before it goes on to packaging.

177
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:20,520
These D-cells output one and a half volts, like all batteries made with the same anode and cathode materials.

178
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,340
But the D-cell's larger size does have its advantages.

179
00:13:25,020 --> 00:13:30,380
The run time of a battery is determined by the amount of active material contained in a battery.

180
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:34,400
Therefore, a D has a lot more potential energy than a AA.

181
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:38,200
This is analogous to a car that has a gas tank.

182
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:41,020
The larger the gas tank, the further a car can run.

183
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:47,680
And speaking of gas tanks, this sleek roadster doesn't have one.

184
00:13:49,020 --> 00:13:55,840
