The advancement in battery technology is based upon new materials.

466
00:34:05,202 --> 00:34:06,400
These new materials are

467
00:34:06,401 --> 00:34:12,180
assembled into small cells which are used for screening tests such as shown here which

468
00:34:12,181 --> 00:34:15,020
we call a button cell.

469
00:34:15,021 --> 00:34:17,500
Because we're working with lithium materials,

470
00:34:17,501 --> 00:34:19,780
lithium reacts violently with water,

471
00:34:19,781 --> 00:34:23,480
either water in liquid form or water in vapor form.

472
00:34:23,481 --> 00:34:27,580
We need an environment where there is very low water vapor,

473
00:34:27,581 --> 00:34:30,340
and therefore we use the glove boxes for this purpose.

474
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:38,560
Another test area was created to work with potentially

475
00:34:38,561 --> 00:34:42,100
volatile battery technologies.

476
00:34:42,101 --> 00:34:45,840
Now these chambers here are designed to minimize those risks.

477
00:34:46,260 --> 00:34:51,540
We have fire detection systems, smoke detection systems, and especially hydrogen detection systems.

478
00:34:51,541 --> 00:34:59,300
If there is release of hydrogen, the ductworks are designed to safely remove the hydrogen from the labs.

479
00:35:01,620 --> 00:35:08,060
One type of battery that Argon researchers are testing is one designed to power the latest generation of hybrid cars.

480
00:35:08,061 --> 00:35:12,160
One of the next steps in hybrid technology is plug-in hybrid vehicles.

481
00:35:12,161 --> 00:35:16,160
And a plug-in hybrid vehicle obviously has a plug that you can plug into the wall.

482
00:35:16,161 --> 00:35:19,660
In addition to a conventional hybrid vehicle, it has a much larger battery, as you can see.

483
00:35:19,661 --> 00:35:23,720
And a larger battery allows you to displace about a gallon's worth of fuel as electricity

484
00:35:23,721 --> 00:35:27,940
each day, so that if you're trying to reduce the amount of petroleum consumption in America,

485
00:35:27,941 --> 00:35:32,320
you can do that with energy charged from the wall at night.

486
00:35:32,321 --> 00:35:36,560
The researchers have two ways to test the plug-in batteries.

487
00:35:36,561 --> 00:35:41,380
They can physically test the car and its battery on a dynamometer, or hook the battery up to

488
00:35:41,381 --> 00:35:45,940
a computer that creates virtual vehicles to put the battery through its paces.

489
00:35:45,941 --> 00:35:51,120
We can put different coefficients into the computer and emulate bigger vehicles, smaller

490
00:35:51,121 --> 00:35:53,940
vehicles, more efficient vehicles, or less efficient vehicles.

491
00:35:53,941 --> 00:35:59,100
We can also change the temperature of the environment by adding or subtracting out heat

492
00:35:59,101 --> 00:36:00,100
from the atmosphere.

493
00:36:00,101 --> 00:36:03,900
The real question we're trying to answer in this facility is, what factors increase the

494
00:36:03,901 --> 00:36:05,800
battery life in a plug-in hybrid vehicle?

495
00:36:06,560 --> 00:36:14,560
2,000 miles from Argonne's research facility, experts at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory

496
00:36:14,561 --> 00:36:19,860
in Pasadena, California are testing batteries for vehicles designed to take us a lot farther

497
00:36:19,861 --> 00:36:22,660
away from home.

498
00:36:22,661 --> 00:36:27,460
If they design anything less than the most long-lasting, durable batteries possible,

499
00:36:27,461 --> 00:36:32,460
a future multi-billion dollar mission could die a lonely death in the farthest reaches

500
00:36:32,461 --> 00:36:33,460
of deep space.

501
00:36:33,461 --> 00:36:40,560
To demonstrate their reliability, a big portion of our activity here at JPL is testing of batteries and cells.

502
00:36:40,980 --> 00:36:48,840
And as you can see in the room behind us, we have a number of environmental chambers in which we have various cells and batteries on tests.

503
00:36:49,320 --> 00:37:00,500
And we're performing primarily cycle life tests that are subjected to the temperature environments that are anticipated or are experienced on spacecraft.

504
00:37:00,501 --> 00:37:05,800
JPL's battery technologies have evolved quickly since the early 90s.

505
00:37:06,860 --> 00:37:13,320
In 1991, nickel-cadmium batteries powered systems on the Topex Earth Orbiter

506
00:37:13,321 --> 00:37:15,460
that mapped ocean topographies.

507
00:37:16,340 --> 00:37:18,520
They were long-lasting but heavy.

508
00:37:19,520 --> 00:37:23,900
In 1997, silver and zinc offered more energy in a lighter package

509
00:37:23,901 --> 00:37:26,740
for the Mars Pathfinder rover Sojourner,

510
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:29,220
but only lasted about 90 days.

511
00:37:30,500 --> 00:37:36,300
Currently, the rovers exploring the Red Planet draw their power from lithium-ion batteries

512
00:37:36,301 --> 00:37:41,300
that are long-lasting, powerful and lightweight.

513
00:37:41,301 --> 00:37:46,200
But like all their predecessors, they also have to push the limits of durability.

514
00:37:46,201 --> 00:37:51,340
In contrast to terrestrial batteries, aerospace batteries really have to withstand the extremes

515
00:37:51,341 --> 00:37:52,900
of space travel.

516
00:37:52,901 --> 00:37:57,600
And for the example of the Mars Exploration Rover batteries, it had to withstand the

517
00:37:57,601 --> 00:38:02,980
launch phase where it is subjected to high shock and vibration levels.

518
00:38:02,981 --> 00:38:04,440
It has to withstand

519
00:38:04,441 --> 00:38:07,183
the vacuum of space on the way to Mars.

520
00:38:07,184 --> 00:38:10,700
And then once it reaches Mars, it has to withstand

521
00:38:10,701 --> 00:38:16,200
the whole entry, descent and landing phase of the mission where it takes another jolt

522
00:38:16,201 --> 00:38:17,020
to the battery.

523
00:38:17,880 --> 00:38:23,700
Because the density of the Martian atmosphere is less than 1% of Earth's, a parachute couldn't

524
00:38:23,701 --> 00:38:26,260
slow down the descent enough to ensure a safe landing.

525
00:38:27,600 --> 00:38:32,760
Just before impact, a set of airbags made out of a strong synthetic material called Vectran,

526
00:38:34,060 --> 00:38:35,760
inflated around the plummeting craft.

527
00:38:36,840 --> 00:38:39,980
Instead of one big damaging jolt to the rover and its batteries,

528
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:42,620
the landing was a series of cushioned bounces.

529
00:38:47,960 --> 00:38:51,980
After surviving the rigorous 34 million mile journey to Mars,

530
00:38:52,840 --> 00:38:57,500
solar panels and the onboard batteries enabled the rovers to poke around the desolate surface.

531
00:38:57,600 --> 00:38:59,060
both day and night.

532
00:38:59,920 --> 00:39:04,360
This is a real-size replica of Mars exploration rover on Mars right now.

533
00:39:04,840 --> 00:39:06,780
And what you see here are these solar panels

534
00:39:06,781 --> 00:39:11,000
that absorb the intense sun radiation.

535
00:39:11,340 --> 00:39:14,500
And then that energy is being fed to the various electronics

536
00:39:14,501 --> 00:39:15,620
to do the science experiments

537
00:39:15,621 --> 00:39:18,660
and also at the same time charging the batteries

538
00:39:18,661 --> 00:39:21,180
that are located in this compartment down below.

539
00:39:22,620 --> 00:39:25,020
After the batteries are charged during the day,

540
00:39:25,021 --> 00:39:28,700
they enable the rovers to continue their scientific chores at night.

541
00:39:30,700 --> 00:39:34,960
However, some of the stored energy is used to power onboard heating systems

542
00:39:34,961 --> 00:39:39,580
that keep the batteries and the electronics from being damaged by nighttime temperatures

543
00:39:39,581 --> 00:39:43,480
that can reach minus 146 degrees Fahrenheit.

544
00:39:45,260 --> 00:39:47,500
Whether we're exploring distant worlds

545
00:39:47,501 --> 00:39:51,920
or just powering up our technology around the house,

546
00:39:52,540 --> 00:39:54,100
one thing is clear.

547
00:39:55,020 --> 00:39:58,560
Our dependence on batteries of all types is continuing to grow.

548
00:39:59,420 --> 00:40:03,080
The dilemma is, what are we going to do with all of our dead batteries?

549
00:40:04,080 --> 00:40:08,860
There are already billions of them in our landfills, and they're poisoning our planet.

550
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:13,620
Current market trends indicate that by the year 2012,

551
00:40:14,520 --> 00:40:18,800
South Korea will account for half of the world's lithium-ion battery production.

552
00:40:19,380 --> 00:40:21,560
Batteries will return on Modern Marvels.

553
00:40:21,561 --> 00:40:27,120
We now return to batteries on Modern Marvels.

554
00:40:29,720 --> 00:40:36,300
No matter how high the quality or the type of battery you purchase, eventually they all kick the bucket.

555
00:40:39,200 --> 00:40:45,880
The chemical reaction that produces the flow of electrons eventually exhausts the supply of internal reactants.

556
00:40:46,680 --> 00:40:49,360
And that's not the only factor determining battery life.

557
00:40:49,361 --> 00:40:54,860
There are a number of things that cause batteries to age.

558
00:40:54,861 --> 00:40:58,360
One of them is simply time.

559
00:40:58,361 --> 00:41:01,040
Temperature has a lot to do with it as well.

560
00:41:01,041 --> 00:41:04,920
Batteries age more quickly at high temperatures.

561
00:41:04,921 --> 00:41:09,100
Rechargeable batteries have a longer lifetime and you can recharge those, but eventually

562
00:41:09,101 --> 00:41:14,080
they die also because the materials inside decompose, there's corrosion, they give off

563
00:41:14,081 --> 00:41:17,760
small amounts of gases, and over the lifetime they lose their charge.

564
00:41:19,360 --> 00:41:27,860
Every year, billions of batteries die, leaving us the enormous problem of what to do with them.

565
00:41:29,200 --> 00:41:34,220
Burying them in our landfills is hardly an answer, as they won't rest in peace.

566
00:41:35,380 --> 00:41:40,140
It's estimated that we recycle only about 2% of household consumer batteries.

567
00:41:41,580 --> 00:41:46,660
Recycling is encouraged for all types, but some batteries pose more of a threat than others.

568
00:41:47,620 --> 00:41:52,020
Those that contain heavy metals, including lead, cadmium, and nickel,

569
00:41:52,540 --> 00:41:55,200
are considered an environmental and public health hazard.

570
00:41:56,600 --> 00:42:02,440
Countless decaying batteries produced prior to a 1996 law restricting the use of mercury as a component

571
00:42:02,441 --> 00:42:05,000
are leaking that toxin into the soil.

572
00:42:06,700 --> 00:42:10,220
We've been a lot more responsible, however, with lead-acid batteries,

573
00:42:10,740 --> 00:42:12,940
like the ones under the hoods of our cars.

574
00:42:12,941 --> 00:42:20,660
Approximately 98% of the lead-acid batteries are returned to recycling facilities to be reused.

575
00:42:22,100 --> 00:42:26,960
The East Penn Manufacturing Company dedicates one entire building to recycling.

576
00:42:28,040 --> 00:42:33,440
Behind me you see 120,000 junk lead-acid batteries that are received here at East Penn.

577
00:42:33,980 --> 00:42:37,740
They begin the process by going up an elevator into a dump station.

578
00:42:37,741 --> 00:42:42,740
When we dump the batteries, they go into two crushers.

579
00:42:42,741 --> 00:42:47,480
The first crusher breaks the batteries into large pieces, allows us to drain the acid out.

580
00:42:48,040 --> 00:42:52,220
From there, the pieces will seed into a hammer mill, where they're sized accordingly.

581
00:42:53,220 --> 00:42:58,360
From there, the battery fragments pass through tumblers and enter float sink tanks.

582
00:43:00,060 --> 00:43:03,040
The plastic from the battery cases floats to the top,

583
00:43:03,041 --> 00:43:10,720
where it's reclaimed and then processed into reusable plastic pellets that will be used to make new cases.

584
00:43:13,720 --> 00:43:18,000
Lead recovered from the bottom of the float sink tanks proceeds through a furnace.

585
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:27,580
The result is a 2,600-pound block of lead that's taken to the refinery,

586
00:43:28,620 --> 00:43:32,260
further refined and delivered to the automotive plants to go into new battery production.

587
00:43:33,040 --> 00:43:36,820
The recycling process produces one bonus byproduct.

588
00:43:37,860 --> 00:43:39,900
Behind me, you can see our scrubber.

589
00:43:40,540 --> 00:43:48,580
The scrubber conditions the gas stream that comes off the furnace and formulates it into a fertilizer stored in our 1 million gallon tanks up on the hill.

590
00:43:49,900 --> 00:43:57,960
The nitrogen sulfur solution made from the fumes given off during the smelting process is sold to agricultural fertilizer manufacturers.

591
00:43:57,961 --> 00:44:04,960
So as strange as it may seem, bumper crops may owe a debt of thanks to the lead-acid recycling process.

592
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:14,960
Beyond recycling, another environmentally positive step was the development of lithium-ion batteries.

593
00:44:15,960 --> 00:44:24,960
Not only do they last much longer than other batteries, the lithium-ion in the electrolyte is an organic salt, and they don't contain any toxic metal.

594
00:44:27,960 --> 00:44:38,960
One thing is sure, those who research and test batteries and those who sell them to us are working hard to keep us all unplugged.

